{"id": "reasoning-f7a07219-24d7-5b30-ac91-785a07ce549e-0", "sessionId": "01f7c834-c499-fb2f-0e3f-33d4d208c428", "turnId": "turn-d4c99f7f7fae63d9ba666324f5523e64", "createdAt": 1792349544667, "updatedAt": 1792349544667, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "There's an `ordinary_restart` concept. At the DC call site (line ~6531), I can pass a new private flag. The cleanest: add keyword `recover_initial_load_factor: bool = False` to `_solve_static_displacement_control`, and at the call site pass:\n\n```python\nrecover_initial_load_factor=(\n    restored_static_path is None\n    and initial_element_states is not None\n    and initial_displacements is not None\n    and equilibrate_initial_state is not True\n),\n```\n\nThat's exactly the \"ordinary supplied initial-state route without re-equilibration\".\n\nNow, the line-search globalization. Where to put it: in the DC Newton loop, after computing delta and before `q += delta[:-1]`. Only on the adaptive route (rotating map, no contact) to preserve legacy/contact behavior. Design:\n\n```python\n                        if adaptive_control_subdivision and np.any(delta):\n                            # Bounded residual line search on the rotating-MPC\n                            # route: full Newton steps can overshoot in the\n                            # postyield regime; backtrack on the augmented\n                            # merit (reduced residual + control constraint)\n                            # while retaining committed histories (trial\n                            # candidates are evaluated, never committed).\n                            ...\n```\n\nMerit function: current merit m0 = residual_norm/reference + constraint_error/target_scale (both computed at current q, lam before the update — we have residual_norm and constraint_error from this iteration's assembly). For alpha trial: q_t = q + alpha*delta[:-1], lam_t = lam + alpha*delta[-1]; u_t = full_u(q_t); assemble F_int (tangent=False) with trial states → discard trial; compute residual_t = reduce(u_t, F_ext_t - F_int_t), current_t = row_full@u_t, constraint_t = target - current_t; merit_t = norm(residual_t)/reference + abs(constraint_t)/target_scale. Accept first alpha with merit_t <= (1 - 1e-4*alpha)*m0? Or simple decrease merit_t < m0. Armijo with small c1=1e-4 is standard. If no alpha in {1, 0.5, ..., 1/256} satisfies, use alpha=1 (unchanged Newton behavior — the iteration then counts as before).\n\nCost concern: each backtrack is an extra assembly without tangent. Assemblies on this small model are fast. Bounded: max 8 halvings.\n\nBut wait — the follower case: F_ext depends on u. On this route follower_active? The EP model has dead loads, so follower_active=False; F_ext = F_const + lam*F_prop independent of u. For generality compute F_ext_t properly: if follower_active, need _weighted_external_load_system at u_t (tangent=False). Keep it general but simple.\n\nAlso trial states: `_assemble_nonlinear_system(model, u_t, committed_states, num_layers, tangent=False, kinematics=..., corotational_tangent=...)` returns (F_int, K, trial_states). The trial must be discarded: `_discard_nonlinear_state_candidate(committed_states)`. Wait — does _assemble_nonlinear_system with committed_states create an \"active candidate\" that needs discarding? The reaction recovery at 4276-4281 does exactly: assemble with committed_states then `_discard_nonlinear_state_candidate(committed_states)`. So yes, same pattern.\n\nHmm, but careful: within the Newton loop, the current iteration's `trial_states` (from the tangent=True assembly) is the active candidate that gets committed on convergence (`states_new = trial_states`). If I call _assemble_nonlinear_system again for the line search, does it REPLACE the active candidate (losing states_new)? The reaction-recovery pattern is used AFTER commit, so no conflict there. Inside the loop, calling assemble again might invalidate `trial_states` (states_new) — the state candidate machinery: `commit_state_candidate(committed, candidate)` — the \"active candidate\" concept: `_discard_nonlinear_state_candidate` calls `discard_active_state_candidate(committed_states)`. So there's a single active candidate slot. If the line-search assembly creates a new active candidate, then `states_new` (from the iteration assembly) might be overwritten/discarded.\n\nLet me check the state candidate machinery to understand. Look at commit_state_candidate / discard_active_state_candidate in the state store module.", "summary": []}
