i)||^2 (e.g., via the barycentric (quaternion-average or projected averaging) iteration), theta_bar = log(R_bar). Connected kinematic map: u_b = sum w_i u_i + (R_bar - I) r_0; theta_b = log(R_bar)... wait, but the slave rotation DOF is a rotation vector; setting theta_b = log(R_bar) makes the slave orientation exactly R_bar. The Jacobian: du_b/dtheta_i involves dR_bar/dtheta_i (the mean's tangent, itself a weighted average in the mean's tangent space with left/right Jacobian weighting), plus the existing lever-arm Jacobian A evaluated at theta_bar = log R_bar. The geometric hessian similarly. The rotation-force covector treatment: reduce_force currently adds delta_A^T f_b to slave theta covectors then applies T_lin^T — with the mean, the slave rotation theta_b = log(R_bar) is a nonlinear function of master rotations, so the map's Jacobian gains a new block: dtheta_b/dq = sum_i W_i dtheta_i/dq where W_i are the mean's weights (tangent-space weights), and the force reduction must include the transpose of that block: master theta covectors get additional terms from the slave moment m_b through the mean's tangent.\n\nThis is the candidate design to propose at the checkpoint. Implementation is substantial; with 450s budget and remaining turns, I'll implement the discriminating check (cheap) and propose the SO(3) mean design, but likely NOT implement the full SO(3) mean route this run — the brief says \"propose an objective SO(3) mean and its connected kinematic map/Jacobian/geometric tangent/rotation-force covector treatment at the early checkpoint\" — propose, and \"An objective mean may replace the nonobjective average on the new active rotating-offset route\" — may. Given budget, proposing with the discriminating evidence is the deliverable; implementing it if time permits.\n\nWait, but also note: the interp model in the test has masters with weights from linear interpolation along x — the two masters of a mid-span beam node. The master shell rotations differ (twist/curvature), so theta_bar averaging is non-objective in 3D.\n\nAlso important: the wrench failure is at 2e-4 relative — could also be just finite-precision? No — 12.8765/61917 = 2.08e-4, way above 1e-6 tolerance and above roundoff for tol 1e-8 solves. It's systematic.\n\nHmm, actually wait. Let me reconsider: is the wrench imbalance caused by non-objectivity of the AVERAGED rotation, or by an inconsistency in transfer_slave_imbalance_to_masters? Look at the transfer: it adds w*(m_b + A^T f_b) to master ROTATION covectors. But the moment covector at the master is a rotation-vector covector, and the physical moment is the spatial moment. The transfer treats master rotation covectors as if they're spatial moments directly weighted (w*m_b). Under finite rotation of the masters, a rotation-vector covector m (conjugate to rotation vector theta) relates to the spatial moment by the left Jacobian... The support wrench computation in the test uses `imbalance[dofs[3:6]]` directly as spatial moments (\"root rotations are fixed zero, so the used moment covectors are legitimate spatial moments\") — at the ROOT, rotations are zero so covectors = spatial moments there. But the transfer to MASTERS (shell edge nodes, not root) treats covectors linearly. The masters' rotations are nonzero; their rotation covectors are not spatial moments. However, the test only uses ROOT node residuals for the support wrench — the transferred master imbalances flow through the shell elements into the root residuals via F_int consistency... no wait, the imbalance vector is nodal forces/moments (covectors) at all nodes; the support wrench sums root-node imbalance components. The shell element internal forces are computed consistently by the corotational formulation, so the full-space imbalance (f_int - f_ext) at equilibrium... but at equilibrium f_int - lam f_ext ≠ 0 in the full space! The MPC slave DOFs carry constraint forces. After transferring slave imbalance to masters, the transferred imbalance should be zero everywhere IF the transfer is consistent and the state is equilibrated... no — the transferred imbalance at masters is w-weighted, and the master nodes' own imbalance is nonzero; the sum is the \"physical\" out-of-balance which should vanish at equilibrium IF the map is the exact derivative of a consistent kinematics and the elements are objective. This is exactly the objectivity question.\n\nOK. I've spent enough on theory; the derivation goes in the checkpoint. Now let me also quickly design the discriminating check concretely:\n\nDiagnostic `diagnostics/rigid_spin_objectivity.py` (new file in task dir? The brief allows diagnostics in the task dir — existing ep_*.py live in the task dir root). Check:\n1. Build the interp EP model (no solve needed — use an arbitrary plausible state, or better, use a small prescribed rotation state).\n2. Build T and the rotating map.\n3. Pick a test state u_lin: give masters distinct finite rotations (e.g., theta1 = [0.05, -0.02, 0.03], theta2 = [0.02, 0.01, -0.04]) and translations; compute u = map.apply_kinematics(u_lin).\n4. Superpose rigid rotation Q = exp(psi^), psi = [0.1, -0.07, 0.13]: u'_lin with master translations transformed as (Q-I)x_i... careful: the map operates on u_lin (linear MPC coordinates) where slave translations get corrected. The equivariance test at the map output level: define the \"deformed attachment point\" x_b = x_b0 + u_b and orientation R_b = R(theta_b). Under superposed rotation about the reference origin, the equivariant image is x_b' = Q x_b, R_b' = Q R_b. Compute the map's output for the spun master state: masters spun: x_i' = Q x_i (u_i' = (Q-I)x_i + ... wait u_i' = Q x_i - x_i = (Q-I)(x_i0 + u_i)? The superposed state has deformed positions Q x_i(deformed). So u_i' = Q(x_i0 + u_i) - x_i0. And theta_i' = log(Q R(theta_i)).\n5. Check: ||x_b(u') - Q x_b(u)|| and ||R_b(u') - Q R_b|| (rotation-vector difference). For direct coupling (single master) these are ~0 (machine precision); for interp with distinct rotations, nonzero → confirms cause (a) at the kinematic level.\n6. Virtual-work covariance: for a random covector... check J(u')^T (Q f) vs the Q-transform of J(u)^T f? The reduced residual lives in q-space (linear MPC coordinates), which is invariant under superposed rotation (q' = q? no...). Hmm, this is subtler. Simpler virtual-work check: the internal virtual work delta W = f^T delta u for spun states. Actually the cleanest second check: wrench covariance — compute the transferred imbalance for the spun state with spun loads and check the support wrench transforms as Q(wrench). If the formulation were objective, wrench'(spun) = Q wrench(original). With the non-objective average, it won't.\n\nBut without an equilibrium solve, the wrench is arbitrary. The check that matters: equivariance of the map's kinematics (attachment point + orientation). That directly tests Astra's adopted contract. If it fails for interp and passes for direct, cause (a) is confirmed as the mechanism violating the adopted contract; the wrench oracle failure follows because the constraint forces (which balance the non-objective kinematics) leave a residual wrench.\n\nHmm, but wait: does the wrench failure appear in DIRECT too? The brief says interp -.10 completed with wrench 12.8765 failing; direct's failures were stalling before target. Did direct's wrench pass at its accepted states? The half direct case (-0.10) presumably completed (stall was at -.1833 in the full run) — and slice3-continuation-achievable-1 had 4 passes which likely included... hmm, 7 failed/4 passed. If half direct passed (including wrench), then the wrench defect is interp-specific → consistent with the averaged-rotation cause (direct has a single master, w=1, exact equivariance). That's already discriminating evidence from the recorded run! Let me check the evidence from slice3-continuation-achievable-1 to see exactly which 4 passed and the failure messages. That's free (no numerical cost). Let me lo