trinsic weighted SO(3) mean:\n\n  R_bar = argmin_R sum_i w_i ||log(R^T R_i)||^2,\n\nby the standard barycentric fixed-point iteration in the mean's tangent\nspace, with theta_bar := log(R_bar). Connected treatment required by the\nmap's other members:\n\n- apply_kinematics/to_linear_displacement: offset (R_bar - I) r_0 and\n  slave orientation theta_b = log(R_bar); the inverse restart map uses the\n  same mean.\n- jacobian: du_b/dq gains dR_bar/dtheta_i through the mean's tangent\n  weights W_i, composed with the existing lever-arm Jacobian A(theta_bar);\n  the slave rotation rows gain sum_i W_i dtheta_i/dq.\n- reduce_force: master rotation covectors receive the transpose of the\n  mean's tangent block applied to the slave moment covector (in addition\n  to the existing delta_A^T f_b term).\n- geometric_tangent: rotating_mpc_geometric_hessian at theta_bar = log\n  R_bar plus the mean-curvature hessian term.\n- transfer_slave_imbalance_to_masters: distribute the slave moment through\n  the mean's tangent weights (not the linear w_i) so the physical wrench\n  oracle stays the transpose of the actual kinematics.\n- Limits: exact for a single master (reduces to direct), exact for\n  identical master rotations, consistent with the linearized weights to\n  first order (small-rotation/public linearized relation unchanged).\n  Constraint postchecks, inverse restart mapping and downstream nonlinear\n  rotation dependency refusals must be re-admitted against the new map.\n\nThis is candidate design within the adopted objective; Astra reviews\nscientific applicability before adoption. No mechanics change is made in\nthis run without that review; the probe only demonstrates the defect.\n\n### Intended remaining edits (this run)\n\n1. DC bounded residual line search on the adaptive rotating-MPC route only\n   (trial evaluations replace the pending state candidate and are never\n   committed; committed histories retained; bounded 8 halvings; fall back\n   to the full Newton step when no reduction improves the merit).\n2. Ordinary-resume lambda recovery as a Newton guess inside\n   _solve_static_displacement_control (least-squares scalar from the copied\n   initial state, rotating route, ordinary supplied initial-state route\n   only; no public API/success-criterion change).\n3. D1 declared-class spy regression (monkeypatched factorize recording\n   MatrixClass and delegating unchanged; independent of optional PARDISO).\n4. Bounded zero-span restart regression (EP fixture, restart at the current\n   controlled value, max_iterations=1: must terminate with a finite failure\n   status, not retry forever).\n5. T2/T3/T4 acceptance-gap closures in the continuation module\n   (every-snapshot reconstruction at achieved lambda with\n   max(norm(J^T lam F),1) normalization, full GP/fiber history comparison\n   at split/milestones/final, named deep copies observed on\n   rejection/cancellation, milestone-based comparison, no empty conditional\n   passes).\n6. Rigid-spin equivariance probe as a diagnostic script + recorded run.\n\n### Actual artifacts at assignment start (from evidence, not plans)\n\n- 31 charged attempts in budget.json; latest was\n  slice3-continuation-achievable-1 (EXIT_1, 39.234439099993324 s,\n  cumulative 1486.0224586999684 s).\n- tests/test_nonlinear_beam_shell_slice3.py: 16 passes\n  (slice3-repairs-5) on the then-current source. Do not rerun unchanged\n  source16.\n- EP continuation module: 7 failed / 4 passed open (full/half interp wrench,\n  full/resume stalls; see junit evidence).\n- DC status: direct postyield stops uz=-0.18333333333333335\n  (lambda2.66796710849, 14 accepts/26 attempts/11 cuts, min-step stop);\n  interp stops uz=-0.1135417. No fold/collapse claim: unscaled augmented\n  condition numbers are not evidence (dimensional scaling adjudication).\n- Augmented factorization already declares MatrixClass.GENERAL (D1 present\n  in product, spy regression missing). Harness v2 with active-phase\n  accounting; Astra owns timeout admission (max120 diagnostic / max600\n  pytest enforced before launch).\n\n## Completed in prior runs (evidence retained; do not repeat)\n\n- Baseline 22 + 39 passes; seven-case plastic attribution (partial-load\n  plateau decided valid); clean-HEAD VK attribution; pre-yield controls;\n  serialized Q4 checkpoint limitation documented (pre-existing).\n- Harness v2; ep_attribution.py prose; R1 corotational guard; R3 n_red==0\n  selector (corrected per supervisor recheck); R4 work_conjugate auto; R5\n  full_u(q) DC admission; tests/test_nonlinear_beam_shell_slice3.py (16 pass).\n- EP continuation module exists with corrected reaction oracle (T1 sound);\n  T2/T3/T4 acceptance gaps open.\n\n## Reserved decisions (for Astra)\n\n- Rigid-spin equivariance contract adopted by Astra; SO(3) mean\n  applicability on the new active rotating-offset route is candidate\n  design, reviewed by Astra.\n- No physics, tolerance, success-criterion or public-contract changes made.\n"}, "outputText": "", "durationMs": 0.0, "display": {"success": true, "verb": "Created", "message": "C:\\Github\\ANYsolver\\.development\\nonlinear-master-slave-coupling-20261017\\checkpoint.md", "warnings": [], "approvalNote": null, "suffix": ""}, "decision": "execute", "approvalType": "always", "approvalSource": "bypass"}}
{"id": "reasoning-deb962e0-ac54-5dd9-8a6f-4dfaf2c33fef-0", "sessionId": "01f7c834-c499-fb2f-0e3f-33d4d208c428", "turnId": "turn-d4c99f7f7fae63d9ba666324f5523e64", "createdAt": 1792349667872, "updatedAt": 1792349667872, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "Early checkpoint written. Now implementation continues. Next steps in order:\n\n1. Line search + lambda recovery in nonlinear_static.py (source edits).\n2. D1 spy test + zero-span test + T2/T3/T4 test edits.\n3. Rigid-spin probe diagnostic.\n4. Numerical runs (within 450s): \n   a. single direct DC test with -x (get actual failure) — but wait, with the line search implemented, the failure may be fixed. The brief says \"First obtain single direct test's actual failure via -x\". Hmm — the brief wants the actual failure observed first. But if I implement the line search first and it fixes the stall, I never see the actual failure. The brief's intent: don't repeat buffered two-route timeouts; get an informative single-test run. The recorded evidence already shows the direct failure mode (max_iterations_reached at min step, from ep-dc-limit-probe-1 and block-probe evidence). The \"actual failure via -x\" is about getting the traceback/failure detail of the TEST (assertion vs error). Actually the direct full test fails with an assertion (status != completed), not a traceback mystery. The brief: \"First obtain single direct test's actual failure via -x, rather than buffered two-route timeout without traceback.\" — i.e., before more diagnosis, run the single direct test to see its actual failure. But the previous run (slice3-continuation-achievable-1) already gave us junit failure messages for half[interp]; the full/resume tests weren't in that -k selection. The 7 failed were from slice3-continuation-1 (earlier, buffered two-route). \n\nGiven the 450s budget, I should be strategic: implement the fixes (line search + lambda recovery), then run the single direct full test with -x. If it passes, run the rest. If it fails, I have the actual failure detail. This satisfies \"obtain single direct test's actual failure\" in the most useful sense — but the brief explicitly orders: first obtain the actual failure via -x, THEN pick globalization. Hmm. \"Pick a compatible internal globalization or predictor (e.g. residual line search retaining committed histories) rather than further load sweeps.\" The order in the brief: \"First obtain single direct test's actual failure via -x ... Use accepted progress in any diagnostic. Pick a compatible internal globalization or predictor\".\n\nThe purpose of getting the actual failure first is to know WHAT fails (assertion on status? which step?). We already know from probes: direct stalls at step 11 milestone -.1833 with maximum_iterations_reached at min sub-step. The probe ep-dc-limit-probe-1 and block-probe-direct-1 recorded exactly this. So the failure mode is known. But the brief is explicit. Cost of a single direct full test: the full 12-step direct solve took... ep-edge-dc-full-direct was 18s (EXIT_1). With subdivision it's longer; block-probe-direct-1 was 88s (that was a probe with more work). A single direct full test with -x: maybe 30-60s. That's affordable within 450s.\n\nBut here's the thing: if I run it BEFORE implementing the line search, I get the baseline failure (known). If I implement first, I might skip that run entirely and save ~40s for the post-fix verification. The brief's instruction exists because previous runs buffered two routes and timed out without traceback. The recorded evidence already contains the direct failure detail (ep-dc-limit-probe-1: maximum_iterations_reached, direct uz-.1667 lambda2.80 alpha.02142; block-probe terminal state -.1833, 14 accepts/26 attempts/11 cuts). I'll treat that as the \"actual failure\" evidence and note it. But to be safe and compliant, I'll run the single direct test with -x AFTER implementing the line search — if it still fails, that IS the actual failure with traceback; if it passes, the failure mode is confirmed resolved and the recorded probe evidence documents the pre-fix failure. Hmm, but then I can't discriminate whether the line search caused the improvement... I can compare against the recorded pre-fix stall (uz=-.1833).\n\nActually, let me reconsider: is the line search the right fix for the direct stall? The stall: Newton with 40 iterations fails even at min step (span/256 ≈ 0.2/12/256 ≈ 6.5e-5 m). At such a tiny control increment, Newton should converge in a few iterations unless the tangent is wrong or the state is at a bifurcation/limit point in lambda. The block probe showed: at the terminal state, tiny-step prediction uses POSITIVE control displacement (unloading) while desired increment is NEGATIVE — the probe had a sign issue. The FD sign error explained relative_error 2.0. \"no source tangent sign repair\" — the source tangent is fine.\n\nWhy would Newton fail at tiny steps? Possibly the augmented system's lambda unknown: at the postyield plateau, du_control/dlambda ≈ -0.0106 m per lambda (from the adjudication). The augmented Newton should handle that. Maybe the issue is the constitutive return map oscillating between elastic/plastic branches (trial straddling yield surface) causing non-convergence at 1e-8 tolerance? Or the geometric tangent of the MPC?\n\nThe line search on the merit (residual + constraint) would help if Newton steps overshoot. With maxiter 40 and tiny steps, overshoot seems unlikely... unless the Newton correction is huge due to an ill-conditioned augmented matrix (near-singular K_red at the plastic plateau). The adjudication says control compliance is comparable to elastic — not a fold. Hmm.\n\nAnother possibility: the lambda recovery. In the FULL run (not resume), lam starts at 0 and evolves; no issue there.\n\nLet me think about what actually happens at step 11 (milestone -.1833): 14 accepts/26 attempts/11 cuts. 11 cutbacks = max_control_cutbacks (8) per interval... 11 cuts total across steps. The min sub-increment = 0.2/12/256 = 6.5e-5. The direct run reached -.1833 = milestone 11 of 12. So step 11 failed at all 8 cutbacks... wait max 8 cutbacks per interval now (before my fix there was no count bound, only the geometric floor: cutbacks while span*0.5 >= min → at most 8 halvings from span=0.0167 down to 6.5e-5 — consistent with 11 cuts over several steps).\n\nSo at step 11, even a 6.5e-5 m control increment fails to converge in 40 Newton iterations. That's really suspicious of something other than step size — with a consistent tangent, a 6.5e-5 increment needs ~2-3 iterations. Unless: the return map at the trial state flips branches (elastic unload/plastic reload) each iteration, causing a limit cycle in the fixed-point... Newton with algorithmic tangent (consistent?) — which tangent does the rotating route use? work_conjugate. The constitutive tangent: is it the consistent (algorithmic) tangent or the elastic one? If the fiber return map uses the continuum tangent, Newton converges linearly — still should converge in 40 iterations for a tiny step unless the yield surface curvature is extreme.\n\nAlternatively, the residual tolerance: tol 1e-8 relative to reference = max(||J^T(F_const + max(|lam|,1) F_prop)||, 1). At lam≈2.7, reference is large; residual_norm must be <= 1e-8 * reference. Fine.\n\nOr: the constraint_error <= tolerance * target_scale with target_scale = max(|target_increment|, |target_total|, 1e-9) = 0.2. So constraint must be <= 2e-9 m. OK.\n\nHmm, what about the \"positive tiny-step probe unloads\" note: the tiny-step prediction at the terminal state with POSITIVE control displacement unloads. That was probe error (wrong sign), not product.\n\nWithout more evidence, the line search is the brief's suggested compatible globalization. Also permitted: \"Ordinary resume lambda recovery from copied equilibrium is permitted as a Newton guess\" — that's for the resume tests.\n\nWait, actually — maybe I should reconsider the failure mode. Let me look at the recorded probe evidence for what the Newton iterations did at the failing step. ep-dc-limit-probe-1 output might show iteration residuals. Let me check the evidence files (free). Also block-probe-direct-1 stdout. Let me look at those to understand before implementing.", "summary": []}
