99999999991773}, {\"column\": 23, \"relative_error\": 1.9999999999967246}, {\"column\": 20, \"relative_error\": 1.9999999999529021}], \"kred_smallest_singular_values\": [81623.26233008593, 42020.96620420906, 21937.9303365796, 13830.91014151572], \"kred_largest_singular_value\": 8488411380.460834}\r\n{\"stage\": \"fd_check\", \"tag\": \"terminal\", \"lam\": 2.667967108494833, \"columns\": [{\"column\": 14, \"relative_error\": 1.9999999999814553}, {\"column\": 15, \"relative_error\": 1.9999999999713178}, {\"column\": 23, \"relative_error\": 1.999999999998481}, {\"column\": 20, \"relative_error\": 1.9999999999543432}], \"kred_smallest_singular_values\": [80863.6030749123, 42476.967321067925, 27020.007979746708, 14357.308890355978], \"kred_largest_singular_value\": 8488416699.615674}\r\n{\"stage\": \"augmented\", \"tag\": \"terminal\", \"aug_smallest_singular_values\": [42490.04725228432, 27022.319180227547, 14365.070730849897, 0.01059206457439015], \"aug_largest_singular_value\": 8488416699.615674, \"tiny_step_dq_norm\": 0.00026641944764223155, \"tiny_step_dlam\": -0.006140727268927975, \"tiny_step_trial_residual\": 6.35197878388043, \"tiny_step_trial_uz\": -0.1832682291666667}\r\n", "durationMs": 0.0, "display": {"success": true, "verb": "Ran", "message": "cd \"C:/Github/ANYsolver/.development/nonlinear-master-slave-coupling-20261017\" && wc -c evidence/ep-dc-block-probe-direct-1/stdout.txt && head -c 3000 evidence/ep-dc-block-probe-direct-1/stdout.txt", "warnings": [], "approvalNote": null, "suffix": ""}, "decision": "execute", "approvalType": "always", "approvalSource": "bypass"}}
{"id": "reasoning-0eed01a8-172a-5a92-92da-bff6b592c3a0-0", "sessionId": "01f7c834-c499-fb2f-0e3f-33d4d208c428", "turnId": "turn-d4c99f7f7fae63d9ba666324f5523e64", "createdAt": 1792349678554, "updatedAt": 1792349678554, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "Key data from block-probe-direct-1:\n- Terminal state: lam 2.668, uz -.1833, 14 accepts, 26 attempts, 11 cuts, 575 total iterations.\n- The tiny-step probe: tiny_step_dq_norm 2.66e-4, dlam -0.00614, trial residual 6.35 (absolute), trial uz -.18327. So a single Newton step at the terminal state toward the next milestone reduces... the trial residual is 6.35 — what's the reference? reference = ||J^T(F_const + max(|lam|,1) F_prop)|| — at lam 2.67, that's ~2.67 * ||J^T F_prop||. The residual 6.35 vs reference... if reference ~ 1e3-1e4, relative ~1e-3 — not converged, needs more iterations.\n\nThe FD check relative_error 2.0 = the probe's sign error (adjudicated).\n\nThe augmented smallest singular value 0.0106 vs largest 8.5e9 — the adjudication says this is dimensional and not fold evidence. The control compliance du/dlam ≈ 6.5e-5/(-6.14e-3) = -0.0106 m/lambda — matches the tiny singular value (the control direction). Elastic control compliance similar → not a fold.\n\nSo Newton at the terminal state takes steps that leave residual 6.35 (absolute). The question is whether subsequent iterations reduce it. 575 total iterations / 26 attempts ≈ 22 iterations per attempt average — attempts end at 40 (max) or accept. The failing attempts hit 40 iterations without reaching 1e-8 relative.\n\nGiven tol 1e-8 (very tight) and slow linear convergence (7 iterations per accepted step at 1e-6-ish relative), near the stall the convergence rate must degrade so that 40 iterations can't reach 1e-8. A residual line search won't fix slow linear convergence. What would? A better tangent (consistent algorithmic tangent — but that's a physics/constitutive change? No — the tangent choice is internal; but \"no physics changes\" — the consistent tangent is not a physics change, it's the exact linearization of the return map; but it IS a constitutive tangent change that Astra may reserve...). Hmm.\n\nWait — actually, let me reconsider. What tangent does the fiber plasticity use? If the element uses the continuum (elastic) tangent during plastic flow, Newton converges linearly with rate ~ E_t/E. For S355 with hardening, rate could be ~0.9+ → 40 iterations gives 0.9^40 ≈ 0.015 — not enough to go from 1e-2 to 1e-8. That matches: steps take 6-8 iterations early (rate better), then fail later.\n\nBut hold on — the earlier accepted steps converged in 6-8 iterations to 1e-8 relative. If the tangent were merely elastic during yield, convergence would be much slower from the start. 6-8 iterations suggests a decent tangent early. The failure at step 11+ suggests something changes: maybe the return map hits the yield apex/knee (sigma_yield_2 transition) where the tangent is discontinuous, or fiber unloading/reloading flips.\n\nAlternatively — the work_conjugate corotational tangent is an approximation (rotated tangent, not exact). Hmm, \"work_conjugate\" is the adopted exact-ish one.\n\nI can't fully diagnose statically. The brief's guidance: \"Pick a compatible internal globalization or predictor (e.g. residual line search retaining committed histories) rather than further load sweeps.\" — Astra explicitly suggests the residual line search. Also permitted: lambda recovery guess. Both are compatible internal changes. The line search helps if iterations oscillate; the recorded evidence doesn't show oscillation, but I don't have iteration-level residual traces. The brief says \"Use accepted progress in any diagnostic\" — i.e., when I run the single direct test, retain per-accepted progress so timeout output is informative.\n\nPlan: implement the line search (bounded, merit-based) + lambda recovery, then run the single direct full test with -x and per-accepted progress. If it still fails, the run output (with line-search info) gives the actual failure. Cost ~40-90s.\n\nActually wait — there's one more consideration on the line search design. If Newton is slowly converging (not diverging), the merit at alpha=1 decreases monotonically, line search accepts alpha=1 always → no change → still fails. If Newton oscillates, line search helps. Given uncertainty, implement it as suggested and let the run discriminate.\n\nAlso — one more possibly-crucial detail: the convergence check `residual_norm <= tolerance * reference and constraint_error <= tolerance * target_scale`. The residual includes BOTH equations? No — residual is only the reduced equilibrium; constraint is checked separately. In the augmented Newton, both are solved simultaneously. Fine.\n\nAnother thought — the reference uses max(abs(lam),1)*F_prop — at lam 2.8, reference is 2.8x the unit-load reduced force. OK.\n\nLet me now also reconsider the lambda recovery for the RESUME tests: resume from half (-0.10, lam 2.56) with lam starting at 0. The first Newton attempt must recover lam 0→2.56 while also moving control -0.10→-0.1167. That's a big first correction; with subdivision it can cut back... but the cutback only reduces the control span, NOT the lambda gap — lam still starts at 0 every attempt! The augmented Newton must recover the full lambda in the first iterations of every attempt. If that's the obstruction, the resume tests fail not because of the control increment but the lambda guess. The permitted lambda recovery fixes exactly this. For the resume route this is likely THE fix.\n\nFor the full run (fresh), lam evolves continuously — no lambda gap. The full-run stall at step 11 is a different mechanism (postyield convergence). Hmm.\n\nBut wait — the full direct run in the test (test_dc_full_target_plasticity_and_equilibrium) uses the same _full_case. The stall at -.1833 is the full run. So the full test needs the stall fixed; the resume test needs both the stall fixed AND the lambda guess.\n\nFor the stall: line search may or may not fix. Let me think harder about what happens at step 11. Actually, let me look at the ep-dc-step11-probe-1 evidence — it probed step 11 specifically.", "summary": []}
